 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "Probably not by much. The latest NEA update, issued 16 October 2026, expects the haze to stay bad tomorrow, with a small chance of some relief.\n\n- **Forecast for 17 Oct:** The PSI forecast for 17 Oct 2026 is 150 – 230 (Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy). That is about the same as tonight. As at 8pm, the 24-hr PSI was 159 – 222, in the Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy range.\n- **Possible relief:** Some showers are expected over Singapore and the surrounding region, including parts of Kalimantan. Winds are forecast to shift to blow from the southeast or south-southeast, so less smoke haze may be transported from Kalimantan to Singapore. The NEA text I retrieved cuts off after \"However,\" so I can't see what caveat it adds.\n- **It could get worse first:** Environment Minister Grace Fu said the PSI might exceed 300 (hazardous) in the coming days because of weather conditions. Oct 16 was the 12th consecutive day of unhealthy PSI readings, the longest stretch of poor air quality since 2015. The article's headline says the haze should improve once the inter-monsoon period starts. It doesn't say when that will be.\n\n**What to do tomorrow:** The NEA advises healthy people to avoid prolonged or strenuous outdoor exertion. The elderly, pregnant women and children should minimise outdoor activity. People with chronic lung or heart disease should avoid outdoor activity.\n\nFor the next day, use the 24-hour PSI forecast to plan ahead. For what you're about to do right now, check the 1-hour PM2.5 reading on the NEA website or the myENV app.",
