     "content": [],
     "return_code": 0,
     "stderr": "",
     "stdout": "\nFollow us on our official WhatsApp channel for breaking news alerts and key updates! Tags / Keywords: SCMP , Singapore , Haze , PSI Readings , Air Quality , Grace Fu , N95 Masks , Monsoon , NEA , PM2.5 , Vulnerable Groups.\n\n# Singapore's PSI could go over 300 before haze improves once inter-monsoon period starts, says environment minister\nThe Republic has endured its longest stretch of poor air quality since 2015.\n\nSINGAPORE (The Straits Times/ANN): The haze in Singapore might reach hazardous levels, with PSI readings exceeding 300, in the coming days due to weather conditions, said Minister for Sustainability and the Environment Grace Fu on Oct 16.\n\nThe Republic has endured its longest stretch of poor air quality since 2015 – Oct 16 marked the 12th consecutive day it registered unhealthy Pollutant Standards Index (PSI) readings.\n\nSingapore last registered PSI readings above 300 in 2015, when it reached as high as 341.\n\n\n\nThe PSI has five bands: good (0-50), moderate (51-100), unhealthy (101-200), very unhealthy (201-300) and hazardous (>300).\n\nAs it is calculated across 24 hours, Fu said the reading is useful for assessing sustained exposure.\n\nPeople should refer to the PSI in tandem with the other measurement of air quality, the hourly PM2.5 readings, she added.\n\nFu said a high 24-hour PSI reading and a low PM2.5 reading indicate that current conditions have improved, although prolonged exposure is still a concern.\n\nConversely, a low 24-hour PSI reading and a high PM2.5 measurement show that conditions have recently worsened, even though the longer-term average has not increased.\n\nAs such, Fu advised the public to look at the 24-hour PSI reading to plan for activities the next day, adding that she had asked the National Environment Agency (NEA) to make the information clearer on its website.\n",
